  "text": "We examine a classical description of charged particles that also carry a magnetic‑type dipole, moving under the influence of an electromagnetic environment. The analysis assumes that the rotational degrees of freedom are not tied to the particle’s translational speed, and that the two invariant magnitudes of the dipole’s six‑component object remain constant over time. Depending on whether the scalar products between the dipole and the total spin six‑vectors stay fixed, two alternative formulations arise; one of them reproduces a well‑known earlier model. Each formulation is cast into a canonical framework by employing the relativistic link between momentum and rest mass, adjusted to include spin‑related kinetic and potential contributions, together with the usual bracket structure for position and spin variables. A special limiting technique is applied to handle the field, and a variational principle is presented using either a Dirac‑type limiting approach or a relativistic cutoff method. Finally, the procedure is extended to the case where the interacting field is a massive vector field, and quantization follows by applying standard correspondence rules."
